fn slice_a() -> Vec<KernelRule> {
    vec![
        // ── Rule 1 (category L — conv-as-GEMM + memory layout, the
        // tile-legality axis): a warp-granularity kernel family's tile
        // height must be a multiple of the PHYSICAL warp size — enforced
        // statically at the instantiation, and the host dispatch
        // predicate must consume the device's warp size, never a
        // compile-time constant. ──
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "physical-warp-size-keys-tile-legality-and-dispatch".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "MECHANISM (tile-geometry legality and dispatch granularity key on the PHYSICAL warp size of the device, never on a compile-time warp-size constant; category L — the tile-legality axis of the memory-layout family): a template kernel family fixes its tile height as a constexpr beside a compile-time warp-size constant, but the physical warp is a DEVICE property — 32 on NVIDIA, 64 on wave64 (RDNA/HIP) — and ONE law has TWO arms. THE STATIC ARM, verbatim (llama.cpp #30837, mmq.cuh — subject 'ggm-cuda: Enforce DP4A MMQ tile height multiple of warp size', body 'Added static assertion to enforce DP4A MMQ tile height requirement.'): the template already reads the real property, 'constexpr int warp_size = ggml_cuda_get_physical_warp_size();', and the one-line patch adds the contract where the tile constant is in scope, 'static_assert(I % warp_size == 0, \"DP4A MMQ tile height must be a multiple of the physical warp size\");' — I is the family's template tile height, the assert lives INSIDE the kernel template, so every (type, J, fallback, prec) instantiation is checked against its own physical warp at compile time. THE RUNTIME ARM, verbatim (llama.cpp #30836 — subject 'HIP/CUDA: Fix reduction in mmf getting tile widths not devisiable by 64 in mul_mat_id on wave64 devices' [sic]): both the dispatch path and its needs-sync predicate gained the device read, 'const int warp_size = ggml_cuda_info().devices[ggml_cuda_get_device()].warp_size;', and the should-use call reads the device, 'if (ggml_cuda_should_use_mmf(src0->type, cc, warp_size, src0->ne, src0->nb, src1->ne[2], /*mul_mat_id=*/true)) {' — replacing the WARP_SIZE argument: a tile-width assumption baked at 32 mis-selected the mmf kernel on wave64 devices, where the reduction's tile width came out not divisible by 64. VERIFY-OUT-OF-LANE ADJUDICATION (the claim's #30836 condition): on NVIDIA the two spellings agree (32 == 32) and the fix is behavior-neutral there — it bites exactly on wave64 (RDNA/HIP); as a LAW it is portable to any target where physical != compile-time, and the corpus pre-B281 mentioned wave64 only as a passing constants-recompute note (bucketed-sparse-reader-column-partition, entries_gpu_15) — no owner. DECISION CONTENT: (a) the legality is PER-INSTANTIATION — the assert sits where the tile constant and the warp constant are both in scope, so a new instantiation that changes the tile height re-checks automatically, at BUILD time; (b) the dispatch predicate and the kernel contract must AGREE on the warp spelling — a predicate passing the constant while the kernel keys on the physical size either refuses a usable kernel or dispatches into an unasserted geometry; (c) the two failure modes are the pair the arms address: unasserted + mis-dispatched = silent banding waste (dead row slots, torn warp-wide passes — 48 rows tile as one 64-wide band with 16 dead slots), asserted = a loud compile refusal at the instantiation, which is the correct arm (refuse at build time what cannot tile at run time); (d) wave64 neutrality is itself part of the claim — where the spellings agree the law costs one alias read. CLASS SIGNATURE: a warp-granularity kernel family (MMQ-class tile kernels, warp-wide reductions, tile epilogues) with a constexpr tile height and a host-side should-use predicate; any device family whose physical warp width differs per device or per backend. REGIME: wave64 RDNA/HIP targets; template families whose tile height changed without re-deriving; host predicates sharing geometry constants with device templates. ANTI-REGIME: single-warp-width ecosystems where the spellings cannot disagree; kernels with no warp-granularity tiling. KINSHIP (do not double-count): swap-ab-decode-operand-roles-tile-legality (entries_gpu_125) owns tile legality as OPERAND-ROLE legality (block-M congruence on the mma path, warp-shape divisibility of the SWAPPED geometry) — this owns the WARP-SIZE GRANULARITY axis (tile vs the device's physical warp); host-dispatch-predicate-mirrors-kernel-compiled-arch (entries_gpu_105) is the sibling law one level up — the host predicate must consume what the kernel actually compiled/believes (ARCH constants there, warp-size constants here: the same 'host and device must read the same constant source' class); bucketed-sparse-reader-column-partition (entries_gpu_15) mentions wave64 only as a constants-recompute caveat, not a legality owner. GREP EVIDENCE: 'physical warp' -> ZERO owners pre-batch; 'tile height' -> ZERO; 'wave64' -> the gpu_15 passing note only; 'static_assert' -> fixture-only hits; 'should_use_mmf' -> ZERO. NotBenchable here (llama.cpp CUDA/HIP sources; the corpus serves no CUDA validator — the legality predicate, the const-instantiation gate and the dispatch selection are CPU-testable and ARE the fixtures). Source: ggml-org/llama.cpp #30837 @ 60341eb74d482a2c (thelittlefireman, merged 2026-10-23) + #30836 @ c295a3d9dcbb41ae (Carl Philipp Klemm, merged 2026-10-20), MIT. Batch 281 / Plan 285 / Research 318.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// ref_shape: the tile contract and the dispatch predicate both\n// key on a COMPILE-TIME warp-size constant. On a device whose\n// physical warp is wider (wave64: 64), a tile height that is not a\n// multiple of the physical warp is admitted, and the kernel's row\n// bands fragment.\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\n/// the compiled-in assumption.\nconst WARP_SIZE: usize = 32;\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy)]\nstruct Device {\n    physical_warp_size: usize, // 64 on wave64 targets\n}\n\n/// the template tile height, fixed at compile time next to WARP_SIZE:\n/// a multiple of the COMPILE-TIME constant (32), NOT of the physical\n/// warp the wave64 device runs (64).\nconst TILE_ROWS: usize = 96;\n\n/// the compile-time legality check: keys on the CONSTANT.\nconst fn tile_rows_ok_constant() -> bool {\n    TILE_ROWS % WARP_SIZE == 0\n}\n\n/// the dispatch predicate: shaped by the same constant.\nfn pick_kernel_constant(_dev: &Device) -> &'static str {\n    if tile_rows_ok_constant() {\n        \"tiled-mmq\"\n    } else {\n        \"generic\"\n    }\n}\n\n/// the kernel's row-band epilogue: every band is one physical warp\n/// wide, so the band count rounds UP to the physical warp.\nfn dead_band_rows(dev: &Device) -> usize {\n    let warp = dev.physical_warp_size;\n    TILE_ROWS.div_ceil(warp) * warp - TILE_ROWS\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_constant_admits_an_illegal_tile_on_wave64() {\n    let dev = Device {\n        physical_warp_size: 64,\n    };\n    // the constant-keyed check PASSES against the wrong warp size\n    assert!(tile_rows_ok_constant());\n    // so dispatch picks the tiled kernel...\n    assert_eq!(pick_kernel_constant(&dev), \"tiled-mmq\");\n    // ...whose row bands fragment: 96 rows run as two 64-wide bands\n    // with 32 dead slots, and no band boundary lands on a warp edge\n    assert_eq!(dead_band_rows(&dev), 32);\n    assert_eq!(TILE_ROWS % dev.physical_warp_size, 32);\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// opt_pattern: TWO arms, ONE law — the tile geometry keys on the\n// PHYSICAL warp size. (a) The static arm: each template\n// instantiation asserts its tile height against ITS OWN warp size at\n// compile time (checked where the instantiation happens, so every\n// substitution re-checks). (b) The runtime arm: the dispatch\n// predicate consumes the DEVICE's warp size, never a compiled-in\n// constant. NVIDIA is behavior-neutral (the spellings agree at 32);\n// the arms bite exactly where physical != compile-time (wave64).\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy)]\nstruct Device {\n    physical_warp_size: usize,\n}\n\n/// the shared legality predicate — the ONE source both arms read.\nconst fn tile_legal(tile_rows: usize, warp: usize) -> bool {\n    warp > 0 && tile_rows % warp == 0\n}\n\n/// the static arm, modeled as a generic instantiation: the legality\n/// const is checked PER SUBSTITUTION when the kernel is used.\nstruct MmqKernel<const TILE_ROWS: usize, const WARP: usize>;\n\nimpl<const TILE_ROWS: usize, const WARP: usize> MmqKernel<TILE_ROWS, WARP> {\n    const LEGAL: () = assert!(\n        tile_legal(TILE_ROWS, WARP),\n        \"tile height must be a multiple of the physical warp size\"\n    );\n\n    fn tile(&self) -> usize {\n        let () = Self::LEGAL; // forces the per-instantiation check\n        TILE_ROWS\n    }\n}\n\n/// the runtime arm: the predicate reads the DEVICE's warp size.\nfn family_usable(tile_rows: usize, dev: &Device) -> bool {\n    tile_legal(tile_rows, dev.physical_warp_size)\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_instantiation_keys_on_its_own_warp() {\n    let w64 = MmqKernel::<64, 64>;\n    let w32 = MmqKernel::<64, 32>;\n    assert_eq!(w64.tile(), 64);\n    assert_eq!(w32.tile(), 64);\n    // a 48-tile divides NO physical warp in this family: the const arm\n    // (compile time) and the predicate (run time) refuse the same set\n    assert!(!tile_legal(48, 32));\n    assert!(!tile_legal(48, 64));\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn wave64_dispatch_consumes_the_device_warp() {\n    let wave64 = Device {\n        physical_warp_size: 64,\n    };\n    let nvidia = Device {\n        physical_warp_size: 32,\n    };\n    // a tile the compile-time constant blesses (a multiple of 32) but\n    // the device's physical warp does not divide: the predicate refuses\n    assert!(!family_usable(96, &wave64)); // 96 % 64 != 0\n    assert!(family_usable(96, &nvidia)); // 96 % 32 == 0\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn nvidia_is_behavior_neutral_where_the_spellings_agree() {\n    let nvidia = Device {\n        physical_warp_size: 32,\n    };\n    assert!(family_usable(64, &nvidia));\n    assert!(family_usable(32, &nvidia));\n}\n".to_string(),
        },
        // ── Rule 2 (category H — MoE dispatch, the mul_mat_id op
        // family): the sentinel-skip slot contract — a negative id skips
        // the slot AND zeroes its result row; the shrunk work set's
        // consumers are re-bounded; the uniform exit is licensed by
        // per-block uniformity; backends without the semantic refuse
        // loud. ──
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "sentinel-id-skip-shrinks-work-set-zero-unowned-rows".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "MECHANISM (the sentinel-skip slot contract for indirect/gathered ops: a negative routing id means no matrix/expert owns this slot — the slot is SKIPPED, its result row is ZEROED, and the four surfaces that moved together upstream all move together; category H — MoE dispatch, the mul_mat_id op family): upstream widened MUL_MAT_ID and ADD_ID with an explicit skip semantic — verbatim (llama.cpp #26631, ggml.h), 'an id of -1 skips the slot and zeroes the matching row of the result' for mul_mat_id and 'an id of -1 adds nothing: dst[i0, i1, i2] = a[i0, i1, i2]' for add_id; ggml.c restates the op contract, 'an id of -1 skips the slot: no matrix is selected, c[:,e,t] is set to zero'. SURFACE 1 — OWNERSHIP AND THE ZERO PASS: the matmul writes ONLY the rows an expert owns, so skipped rows need a dedicated pass, never stale memory — verbatim (metal) 'kernel_mul_mm_id only writes the rows that an expert owns, so the skipped rows are zeroed here' + (metal ops) 'zero the rows that no expert owns - disjoint from what kernel_mul_mm_id writes' + (hexagon) 'the matmuls only write the rows that an expert owns, so the rows of the skipped slots are zeroed here'; the CPU reference is the minimal form, 'assert(i02 == -1 || (i02 >= 0 && i02 < n_as));' then 'memset((char *) dst->data + id*nb1 + iid1*nb2, 0, ne0*sizeof(float));' before the 'continue'. SURFACE 2 — RE-BOUND: the work set SHRINKS, so every consumer of the compact map stays in bounds on the shrunk tail — (metal map0) 'sids[i20] = id == -1 ? 0xFFFF : (uint16_t) id;' with '0xFFFF marks a skipped slot, it never matches an expert since ne02 fits in a threadgroup'; (vulkan) 'expert_id = expert_skip ? 0 : uint(expert); // clamp, so that the offsets below stay in range' with the mask at reduce, 'if (expert_skip) { temp[j][n] = FLOAT_TYPE(0); }' (the clamp-then-mask spelling lands as a rider on clamp-tile-indices-to-sentinel-discard-at-write); (SYCL) 'routed_row_src.resize(expert_row_offsets[n_as]);' — the routed set truncated to the VALID count. Three spellings, one law: sentinel-encode, clamp, or truncate — the shrunk set is never consumed at its dense length. SURFACE 3 — UNIFORM EXIT: the skip is per-block uniform (one slot per block/one token per warp), so the whole unit leaves after zeroing — (mmvf) 'channel_x is uniform in the block, so the whole block leaves on a skipped slot'; (mmvq) 'the selected expert is uniform in the block, so the whole block leaves on a skipped slot' and 'each warp owns one token, so the whole warp leaves on a skipped slot'. SURFACE 4 — ADOPTION BOUNDARY: backends without the semantic are marked NOT_SUPPORTED at the test layer, never silently wrong — patch 7/8 EXCLUDED CUDA/ROCm/MUSA from the PR, and the harness refuses the shape loud: 'TODO: [TAG_MUL_MAT_ID_SKIP] @ngxson : remove this once CUDA implements mul_mat_id with -1 = skipped'. DECISION CONTENT: (a) skip-and-ZERO is a CONTRACT, not an optimization — the zero is load-bearing (any consumer of an unowned row must read zero, never the previous call's bytes; the zero pass is the feature); (b) the compact-map consumers re-derive for the shrunk set — dense-length consumption of a shrunk map reads a stale tail row; (c) the uniform-exit license is the UNIFORMITY: the slot id is a per-block constant, so the block-level branch is uniform and the early exit is legal — a per-lane skip would not license it (and where a phase barrier follows, see all-threads-reach-workgroup-barrier-no-pre-barrier-return: the uniform exit must clear the whole group, the gate moves to the stores); (d) the adoption boundary fails LOUD — a lagging backend is excluded from the skip-bearing test cases and tagged, which converts a silent-wrong-answers bug into a visible coverage gap. CLASS SIGNATURE: an indirect matmul / gathered op with a routing id tensor where some slots may be empty (MoE top-k with dropped or padded experts, pruned rows); a compact/gather prepass feeding a matmul that writes only owned rows. REGIME: MoE routing with expert-drop slots; batches pruned between router and matmul; any id tensor where -1 carries meaning. ANTI-REGIME: id tensors guaranteed dense (no skip axis); wrap-the-table semantics (the kinship's other arm); ops whose result rows are always fully written. KINSHIP (do not double-count): gather-signed-index-wrap-semantics (entries_gpu_72) owns the WRAP semantic — a negative SIGNED id wraps to the END of the table (row = id + V) and the row still computes; THIS owns the SKIP semantic (no row selected, zeroed output) — the two are the signed-id family's two arms and a port that confuses them ships wrong rows; moe-duplicate-id-compact-prepass-static-bound-tile-dispatch (entries_gpu_88) owns the compact-prepass STRUCTURE (two-pass count+fill, static bound, tail early-exit, the duplicate-id defect) — THIS owns what a sentinel ID MEANS at the op boundary and its zeroing/re-bound propagation; clamp-tile-indices-to-sentinel-discard-at-write (entries_gpu_01) owns clamp-at-write for tile indices — the vulkan clamp-then-mask face is its op-level instance (rider); moe-unverified-rows-route-zero-experts-dense-shape (entries_gpu_117) owns rows routing zero EXPERTS under the dense shape — the grid-axis sibling (whole rows absent vs slots skipped); batch-composition-invariant-grid-early-return (entries_gpu_14) owns per-row work extents and early return for BATCH padding — a different axis. GREP EVIDENCE: 'skipped slot' -> ZERO owners pre-batch; 'skips the slot' -> ZERO; 'sentinel' hits are pad/list classes (sparse-kv-index-list-sentinel-padded owns sentinel-PADDING an index list, not the op-level skip semantic); '-1' as a routing semantic -> gather-signed-index-wrap only. NotBenchable here (llama.cpp multi-backend sources; the zero-pass, re-bound and uniformity laws are CPU-testable and ARE the fixtures). Source: ggml-org/llama.cpp #26631 (ngxson; 8-patch series head 3332eff1fb170c18, merged 2026-10-24; patch 4/8 is a cherry-pick of d8a4b9ef), MIT. Batch 281 / Plan 285 / Research 318.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// ref_shape: the naive guard on an indirect matmul with skip\n// slots — the loop 'continue's past id == -1, but NOTHING writes the\n// unowned result rows: they keep the caller's stale bytes. And the\n// compact work map lives in a REUSED buffer consumed at its DENSE\n// length: a shrunk set's tail holds the previous call's rows.\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\n/// the work map lives in a REUSED buffer (the upstream ids_src1 pool):\n/// the builder excludes skip slots, writes the valid prefix, and\n/// returns the shrunk count — the tail keeps the previous call's rows.\nfn compact_into(ids: &[i32], buf: &mut [(usize, i32)]) -> usize {\n    let mut n = 0;\n    for (slot, &id) in ids.iter().enumerate() {\n        if id >= 0 {\n            buf[n] = (slot, id);\n            n += 1;\n        }\n    }\n    n\n}\n\n/// ids[slot] = expert, or -1 = skip. The matmul writes only the rows\n/// an expert owns — the naive arm never zeroes the rest.\nfn mul_mat_id_naive(dst: &mut [Vec<f32>], ids: &[i32], experts: &[&[f32]]) {\n    for (slot, &id) in ids.iter().enumerate() {\n        if id < 0 {\n            continue; // skip the slot — the row is NEVER written\n        }\n        dst[slot].copy_from_slice(experts[id as usize]);\n    }\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn unowned_rows_keep_stale_bytes() {\n    // dst rows persist across calls: this turn's caller buffer still\n    // holds the PREVIOUS turn's outputs\n    let mut dst = vec![vec![7.5f32; 2]; 3];\n    let experts = [vec![1.0, 1.0], vec![2.0, 2.0], vec![3.0, 3.0]];\n    let e: Vec<&[f32]> = experts.iter().map(|v| v.as_slice()).collect();\n    // slot 1 is skipped this turn\n    mul_mat_id_naive(&mut dst, &[0, -1, 2], &e);\n    assert_eq!(dst[0], vec![1.0, 1.0]);\n    assert_eq!(dst[2], vec![3.0, 3.0]);\n    // slot 1's row is NOT zero — it is the previous call's bytes: the\n    // contract (skipped slot => zeroed row) is violated\n    assert_eq!(dst[1], vec![7.5, 7.5]);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_shrunk_set_consumed_dense_reads_a_stale_tail() {\n    let mut buf = vec![(9usize, 9i32); 4]; // reused across calls\n    // turn 1: a dense id set fills the prefix\n    assert_eq!(compact_into(&[0, 1, 2], &mut buf), 3);\n    // turn 2: skips shrink the set to one entry; the tail keeps turn 1\n    let n = compact_into(&[0, -1, -1], &mut buf);\n    assert_eq!(n, 1);\n    // a dense-length consumer (walking 3 entries as last turn) reads\n    // buf[1]: turn 1's work item — a STALE row index this call never\n    // wrote; the shrunk set must be re-bounded or its tail re-pointed\n    assert_eq!(buf[1], (1, 1));\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// opt_pattern: the skip contract, all four surfaces —\n// (1) the op contract: an id of -1 skips the slot AND zeroes its\n//     result row (a dedicated zero pass — the matmul writes only\n//     expert-owned rows);\n// (2) the compact map re-bound: the shrunk work set is TRUNCATED to\n//     the valid count and its tail padded (point-at-row-0) so any\n//     dense-length consumer stays in bounds;\n// (3) the uniform exit: the skipped slot is per-block uniform, so the\n//     whole block zeroes its row and leaves;\n// (4) the adoption boundary: a backend without the semantic refuses\n//     loud (NOT_SUPPORTED), never silently wrong.\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\nfn mul_mat_id_skip_contract(\n    dst: &mut [Vec<f32>],\n    ids: &[i32],\n    experts: &[&[f32]],\n    supports_skip: bool,\n) -> Result<(), &'static str> {\n    // (4) the adoption boundary: refuse loud before touching memory\n    if !supports_skip && ids.iter().any(|&id| id == -1) {\n        return Err(\"backend lacks the -1-skip semantic\");\n    }\n    // (1) the zero pass FIRST: the unowned rows are zeroed, not stale\n    for (slot, &id) in ids.iter().enumerate() {\n        if id == -1 {\n            dst[slot].fill(0.0);\n        }\n    }\n    // (2) the compact set: truncated to the valid count, the tail\n    // padded to point at row 0 so dense-length consumers stay in bounds\n    let mut map: Vec<(usize, usize)> = ids\n        .iter()\n        .enumerate()\n        .filter(|&(_, &id)| id >= 0)\n        .map(|(slot, &id)| (slot, id as usize))\n        .collect();\n    let valid = map.len();\n    map.resize(ids.len(), (0, 0));\n    // the matmul over the compact set only\n    for &(slot, expert) in &map[..valid] {\n        dst[slot].copy_from_slice(experts[expert]);\n    }\n    Ok(())\n}\n\n/// (3) the vec kernel's uniform exit: one slot per block, so the skip\n/// is block-uniform — the whole block zeroes its row and leaves.\nfn vec_block(slot_id: i32, row: &mut [f32]) -> bool {\n    if slot_id == -1 {\n        row.fill(0.0);\n        return false; // whole block leaves\n    }\n    true\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn skipped_rows_are_zeroed_not_stale() {\n    let mut dst = vec![vec![7.5f32; 2]; 3];\n    let experts = [vec![1.0, 1.0], vec![2.0, 2.0], vec![3.0, 3.0]];\n    let e: Vec<&[f32]> = experts.iter().map(|v| v.as_slice()).collect();\n    mul_mat_id_skip_contract(&mut dst, &[0, -1, 2], &e, true).unwrap();\n    assert_eq!(dst[0], vec![1.0, 1.0]);\n    assert_eq!(dst[2], vec![3.0, 3.0]);\n    // the skipped row is ZERO — the contract, never stale memory\n    assert_eq!(dst[1], vec![0.0, 0.0]);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_padded_tail_keeps_dense_consumers_in_bounds() {\n    let ids = [0, -1, 1];\n    let mut map: Vec<(usize, usize)> = ids\n        .iter()\n        .enumerate()\n        .filter(|&(_, &id)| id >= 0)\n        .map(|(slot, &id)| (slot, id as usize))\n        .collect();\n    map.resize(ids.len(), (0, 0));\n    // even a consumer that walks the DENSE length reads row 0, in bounds\n    assert_eq!(map[ids.len() - 1].1, 0);\n    assert_eq!(map.len(), 3);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_whole_block_leaves_on_a_uniform_skip() {\n    let mut row = [5.0f32; 4];\n    assert!(!vec_block(-1, &mut row));\n    assert_eq!(row, [0.0; 4]);\n    let mut row = [5.0f32; 4];\n    assert!(vec_block(2, &mut row));\n    assert_eq!(row, [5.0; 4]);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn a_backend_without_the_semantic_refuses_loud() {\n    let mut dst = vec![vec![0.0f32; 2]; 2];\n    let experts = [vec![1.0, 1.0]];\n    let e: Vec<&[f32]> = experts.iter().map(|v| v.as_slice()).collect();\n    let result = mul_mat_id_skip_contract(&mut dst, &[0, -1], &e, false);\n    assert!(result.is_err()); // fail-loud, never silently wrong\n    // and the refusal left memory untouched\n    assert_eq!(dst[0], vec![0.0, 0.0]);\n}\n".to_string(),
        },
        // ── Rule 3 (category E — parallelism): workgroup-barrier
        // participation is group-universal — no lane returns before the
        // barrier; the work-presence gate moves to the post-barrier
        // stores (and optionally the accumulate). ──
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "all-threads-reach-workgroup-barrier-no-pre-barrier-return".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "MECHANISM (workgroup-barrier participation is group-universal: NO lane returns before the barrier — the work-presence gate moves to the post-barrier stores; category E — parallelism): a compute-shader workgroup barrier is a RENDEZVOUS — every thread of the group must reach it — so an early return on a per-lane predicate (this lane found no work) never delivers all arrivals and the group waits forever. THE UPSTREAM FACE, verbatim (llama.cpp #26631 patch 4/8 — 'ggml-webgpu: ensure all threads reach workgroup barriers in mul_mat_id_vec', a cherry-pick of d8a4b9ef): the buggy form was the comment 'skipped slots shrink the gathered set, so excess workgroups have nothing to do' guarding 'if (!found) { return; }' — deleted; replaced by the found gate on the ACCUMULATE, 'if (found) { acc = accumulate_vec_dot(thread_id, row_base, src0_batch_offset, src1_idx_base); }', and the found gate moved onto the STORES after the rendezvous, 'if (found && subgroup_invocation_id == 0) {' and 'if (found && thread_id < OUTPUTS_PER_WG) {' — every lane still walks the whole phase; only the writes are conditional. DECISION CONTENT: (a) the gate belongs on STORES, never on ARRIVALS — a lane that writes nothing still participates in the phase it is in; (b) the ACCUMULATE may also be gated (a not-found lane accumulates nothing) but the PHASE STRUCTURE — the barrier count — may not; (c) the law is language-level (WGSL workgroupBarrier, MSL threadgroup_barrier, HLSL GroupMemoryBarrierWithGroupSync, SPIR-V OpControlBarrier): non-uniform control flow around a group barrier is undefined or a hang, a contract of the languages, not a style choice; (d) this is the lane-level sibling of the block-uniform exit (sentinel-id-skip-shrinks-work-set-zero-unowned-rows): a BLOCK-UNIFORM skip licenses a whole-block early exit before any phase nothing waits on, a LANE-LOCAL predicate never does — the two rules together draw the exact line (uniform across the group = exit; varies within the group = gate the stores). CLASS SIGNATURE: a workgroup-phase kernel (accumulate, barrier, elected store) where work presence is data-dependent per lane or per subgroup. REGIME: WGSL/MSL/HLSL compute kernels with group barriers and data-dependent work; subgroup-elected stores after group phases; any port adding a skip/guard predicate inside a barriered phase. ANTI-REGIME: barrier-free kernels (no rendezvous — early return is fine); group-UNIFORM predicates (a whole-group skip may exit before the phase — the sentinel-skip rule's arm). KINSHIP (do not double-count): loop-back-edge-scratch-war-trailing-warp-barrier (entries_gpu_108) owns barrier PLACEMENT against a WAR hazard on a loop back edge (the trailing barrier protects scratch reuse) — placement for correctness of reuse, not arrival universality; smem-slot-role-reversal-needs-barrier owns a barrier requirement for slot-role reversal; bounded-cross-worker-barrier-protocol-polling-fan-in-ladder owns a HOST-side cross-worker barrier protocol — a different tier; moe-readiness-queue-no-grid-barrier-producer-scans-before-retire (entries_gpu_87) owns REMOVING a grid barrier by restructuring (producer scans before retire) — the inverse move at the grid tier; broadcast-uniform-skip-reduce (entries_gpu_02) owns post-REDUCTION uniformity (a broadcast value re-reduced is multiplied by the block size) — a different invariant about uniformity. NONE owns arrival universality. GREP EVIDENCE: 'workgroupBarrier' -> ZERO; 'workgroup barrier' as a participation law -> ZERO; 'early return' rules are the batch-extent and ownership classes (batch-composition-invariant-grid-early-return entries_gpu_14, the sentinel-skip rule this batch); 'barrier' rules are placement/reuse classes named above. NotBenchable here (the law is a hang/UB contract — the fixture models arrivals deterministically and asserts the deadlock and the repair). Source: ggml-org/llama.cpp #26631 patch 4/8 (yomaytk; cherry-pick of d8a4b9efb6e2b9abf3a866e847756b7e6bbc6b69, merged 2026-10-24), MIT. Batch 281 / Plan 285 / Research 318.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// ref_shape: lanes that find no work RETURN before the group\n// barrier. In WGSL/MSL a barrier is a rendezvous: EVERY thread of\n// the group must reach it. The early return means the barrier never\n// sees all arrivals — a device-side hang: the rest of the group\n// waits forever.\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\n#[derive(Debug, PartialEq)]\nenum Barrier {\n    Released,\n    WouldDeadlock,\n}\n\n/// one workgroup phase: lanes with work accumulate and arrive at the\n/// shared barrier; the barrier needs EVERY lane's arrival.\nfn phase_buggy(found: &[bool]) -> (Barrier, Vec<Option<u32>>) {\n    let size = found.len();\n    let mut arrivals = 0usize;\n    let mut dst: Vec<Option<u32>> = vec![None; size];\n    for (lane, &f) in found.iter().enumerate() {\n        if !f {\n            continue; // the pre-barrier early return\n        }\n        arrivals += 1; // this lane reached the barrier\n        dst[lane] = Some(1);\n    }\n    let barrier = if arrivals == size {\n        Barrier::Released\n    } else {\n        Barrier::WouldDeadlock\n    };\n    (barrier, dst)\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_pre_barrier_return_deadlocks_the_group() {\n    // two of four lanes find nothing and never arrive\n    let (barrier, _dst) = phase_buggy(&[true, false, true, false]);\n    assert_eq!(barrier, Barrier::WouldDeadlock);\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// opt_pattern: NO return before the barrier — every lane arrives,\n// and the work-presence gate moves to the STORES after the rendezvous\n// (and optionally to the accumulate: a not-found lane accumulates\n// nothing but still walks the phase).\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\n#[derive(Debug, PartialEq)]\nenum Barrier {\n    Released,\n    WouldDeadlock,\n}\n\nfn phase_fixed(found: &[bool]) -> (Barrier, Vec<Option<u32>>) {\n    let size = found.len();\n    let mut arrivals = 0usize;\n    let mut dst: Vec<Option<u32>> = vec![None; size];\n    for (lane, &f) in found.iter().enumerate() {\n        arrivals += 1; // EVERY lane reaches the barrier\n        if f {\n            dst[lane] = Some(1); // the store is gated, not the arrival\n        }\n    }\n    let barrier = if arrivals == size {\n        Barrier::Released\n    } else {\n        Barrier::WouldDeadlock\n    };\n    (barrier, dst)\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn every_lane_arrives_and_the_stores_are_gated() {\n    let (barrier, dst) = phase_fixed(&[true, false, true, false]);\n    assert_eq!(barrier, Barrier::Released);\n    assert_eq!(dst, vec![Some(1), None, Some(1), None]);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn an_all_skip_group_still_releases() {\n    let (barrier, dst) = phase_fixed(&[false, false]);\n    assert_eq!(barrier, Barrier::Released);\n    assert_eq!(dst, vec![None, None]);\n}\n".to_string(),
        },
    ]
}

pub fn entries() -> Vec<KernelRule> {
    let v = slice_a();
    debug_assert_eq!(v.len(), 3, "batch 281: 3 own kernel_opt rules");
    v
}
